    pr_ref:  # 5ccf
    description: "FILED 2026-09-09 from WI-sigog's implementation.\n\nscala.py's external METHOD-call branch hand-rolls `Edge.create` where every\nsibling analyzer -- and both of scala.py's own other unresolved-edge sites, 20\nand 90 lines below it -- calls the shared `make_unresolved_edge`. The branch is\nthe odd one out in its own file.\n\nWHAT THE BYPASS COSTS, both of which WI-sigog then had to re-implement by hand:\n\n  * `meta[\"callee_name\"]`, the ADR-0036 ruling-1 LOSSLESS HOME FOR THE NAME.\n    The id's name slot is deliberately lossy, and consumers are instructed to\n    read the name from elsewhere and never re-derive it from the id. For this\n    branch there is no elsewhere. This is the INV-divuf / WI-nakut defect that\n    made an Objective-C selector appear NOWHERE in the output.\n  * the ADR-0037 ruling-2 `dst_ref` derivation, including the WI-huzuv rule that\n    a `dst_ref` is withheld when the module is the `external` sentinel.\n\nWHY IT WAS NOT FOLDED INTO WI-sigog: `make_unresolved_edge` stamps\n`evidence_type=\"ast_call_direct\"` (confidence 0.50) and this branch deliberately\nstamps `ast_call` (0.40) as INV-fahub/WI-bihit bias-to-unresolved. Adopting the\nhelper therefore RE-PRICES 40,970 edges on sbt+lila alone, and bundling a\nconfidence move into a recall fix makes the A/B unattributable -- so WI-sigog\nkept `Edge.create` and its own test pins 0.40 explicitly.\n\nTHE QUESTION THIS ITEM MUST ANSWER FIRST, before any code: is the 0.40 still\nwanted now that the branch carries a receiver TYPE for some of its edges? A\ntyped external method call has strictly more evidence than an untyped one and\nthey are currently priced identically. Either answer is fine; it needs deciding\nrather than inheriting."  # 5ccf
    fields: {}  # 5ccf
- op: discuss  # 0acb
  at: '2026-09-10T01:32:52Z'  # 0acb
