  clock: 2  # 0acb
  nonce: 0acb  # 0acb
  message: "THIS IS A FAMILY OF AT LEAST TWO, NOT A SCALA ONE-OFF. Found 2026-09-09 while\nsizing WI-dodop.\n\nswift.py's external method-call branch hand-rolls `Edge.create` the same way,\nbuilding the dst id inline as\n`f\"swift:{_module or 'external'}:0-0:{callee_name}:unresolved\"` and stamping a\n`gate_meta` that carries `call_construct` and `receiver_type_hint` but NOT\n`callee_name`. Measured on vapor: **all 2,832 external method-call edges with no\nreceiver type carry `meta[\"callee_name\"] = None`**, and so do the rest of the\n4,944.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, and it is not theoretical: a consumer that follows ADR-0036 ruling 1\n-- read the name from `meta[\"callee_name\"]`, NEVER re-derive it from the id --\ngets None for every swift edge and must do the banned thing to get a name at all.\nWI-dodop's own filed callee list (get 239, test 167, decode 85) could not be\nreproduced from metadata for exactly this reason, which is how it surfaced.\n\nObjc is the counter-example in the same measurement: it routes through\n`make_unresolved_edge`, stamps `callee_name`, and its names read back cleanly\neven for selectors ending in a colon (the case INV-divuf / WI-nakut exist for).\n\nSo the fix shape filed here for scala applies unchanged to swift, and whoever\ntakes it should take both -- and check the remaining tree-sitter analyzers for\nthe same bypass rather than assuming it is two."  # 0acb
